digraph ReasoningTree {
  // paper_id=s41467-025-56856-0
  rankdir="TB";
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This paper proposes and evaluates a programmable synthesis-and-selection method for oligonucleotide subset access that reads short barcodes on immobilized DNA at single-nucleotide resolution using reversible and irreversible terminator nucleotides, aiming to avoid per-subset primers and enable scalable hierarchical selection of many subsets in complex oligo libraries.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) When existing primer- or hybridization-based oligo-selection strategies impose scaling overhead, a short-barcode, cyclic single-nucleotide selection mechanism can be investigated as a programmable alternative for subset access."];
  "R1" [label="(0,0,0) Current hybridization- and primer-based oligo-selection approaches impose scaling burdens because they rely on unique hybridization regions and, in multiplex PCR, require primer synthesis that grows with the number of target subsets."];
  "R10" [label="(0,0,0) A barcode-based single-nucleotide-resolution selection strategy is a motivated alternative to per-subset primer addressing because it could address many subsets with short N-nucleotide barcodes while avoiding per-subset primer-pair synthesis."];
  "R11" [label="(0,0,0) Because synthesis-and-selection proceeds through barcode positions and higher-level hierarchical groups can be selected with fewer cycles, partial cycling through a barcode can retrieve grouped subsets sharing the specified prefix."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) Specifying barcode positions through successive synthesis-and-selection cycles can select a particular barcode-defined subset at single-nucleotide resolution."];
  "R13" [label="(0,0,0) PCR-based oligo subset selection has primer-related scaling overhead because it pairs each subset with a primer set and also requires universal-primer replication regions to prevent library depletion."];
  "R3" [label="(0,0,0) An N-nucleotide barcode can in principle encode up to 4^N identifiers, so if such barcodes are read at single-nucleotide resolution, many subsets could be addressed using N barcode nucleotides rather than long per-subset primer regions."];
  "R4" [label="(0,0,0) A selection procedure that accepts matching bases and blocks non-matching bases at each barcode position provides a plausible mechanism for reading barcodes at single-nucleotide resolution without designing a unique primer pair for every subset."];
  "R5" [label="(0,0,0) If cyclic selection repeatedly deprotects matched reversible terminators while non-matching oligos receive irreversible terminators, then oligos with the target barcode remain extendable and can be retrieved."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) PCR is an established approach for selectively amplifying target oligonucleotides from complex libraries using specific primer sequences."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) Because PCR-based subset selection requires unique primer-hybridization regions and primer synthesis burden increases with the number of target subsets, scalable oligo selection is motivated to avoid per-subset primer-pair synthesis."];
  "n1" [label="(1,0,0) Currently, PCR is widely used to selectively amplify target oligonucleotides in complex libraries, and targets are identified by specific primer sequences."];
  "n11" [label="(11,0,0) Currently, multiplexed selection (e.g., multiplex PCR) aims to select multiple subsets at once, but primer synthesis still grows with the number of target subsets and becomes a bottleneck."];
  "n12" [label="(16,0,0) Currently, each nucleotide position has four possible bases, so an N-nt code can, in principle, encode 4^N distinct identifiers (barcodes)."];
  "n16" [label="(19,0,0) Currently, because the synthesis-and-selection approach uses cyclic reactions, selecting higher-level groups in a hierarchy can be done with fewer cycles."];
  "n19" [label="(22,0,0) Currently, in PCR-based selection, there is a one-to-one correspondence between a primer set and an oligo subset, giving a planar (non-hierarchical) selection structure."];
  "n2" [label="(2,0,0) Currently, PCR can selectively amplify targets (biological DNA and synthetic oligos) even from libraries with millions of distinct sequences."];
  "n20" [label="(30,0,0) Currently, during synthesis-and-selection, nucleotides matching the target barcode are coupled with reversible terminators, while non-matching nucleotides are coupled with irreversible terminators."];
  "n22" [label="(39,0,0) Currently, oligos not matching the target barcode receive irreversible terminators and become incapable of further reactions (they are blocked from continued selection cycles)."];
  "n24" [label="(0,0,0) If an N-nt barcode can uniquely label up to 4^N subsets and a method can read that barcode at single-nucleotide resolution, then it should be possible to select many subsets using only N nucleotides rather than long primer-defined regions."];
  "n3" [label="(3,0,0) Currently, for effective PCR each target subset must have a unique primer-hybridization (selection) region; primer pairs are ~20 nt each, so the selection region must support specific hybridization."];
  "n5" [label="(5,0,0) Currently, users must find selection regions and synthesize corresponding primer pairs, and the primer synthesis burden increases in proportion to the number of subsets."];
  "n6" [label="(6,0,0) Currently, many other enrichment/selection methods (e.g., hybrid capture, CRISPR) also rely on hybridization and similarly depend on unique-region length."];
  "n9" [label="(9,0,0) Currently, preventing library depletion requires library amplification, which requires an additional ~40 nt universal-primer replication region."];
  "n6" -> "R1" [label="induction-case"];
  "n11" -> "R1" [label="induction-common"];
  "n1" -> "R7" [label="abduction-phenomenon"];
  "n2" -> "R7" [label="abduction-knowledge"];
  "n24" -> "R3" [label="deduction-rule"];
  "n12" -> "R3" [label="deduction-case"];
  "n5" -> "R8" [label="deduction-rule"];
  "n3" -> "R8" [label="deduction-case"];
  "R8" -> "R10" [label="deduction-rule"];
  "R3" -> "R10" [label="deduction-case"];
  "R3" -> "R4" [label="abduction-phenomenon"];
  "n20" -> "R4" [label="abduction-knowledge"];
  "R4" -> "R5" [label="deduction-rule"];
  "n22" -> "R5" [label="deduction-case"];
  "n16" -> "R11" [label="deduction-rule"];
  "n20" -> "R11" [label="deduction-case"];
  "n24" -> "R12" [label="deduction-rule"];
  "n20" -> "R12" [label="deduction-case"];
  "n19" -> "R13" [label="abduction-phenomenon"];
  "n9" -> "R13" [label="abduction-knowledge"];
  "R1" -> "NROOT" [label="induction-case"];
  "R10" -> "NROOT" [label="induction-case"];
  "R11" -> "NROOT" [label="induction-case"];
  "R12" -> "NROOT" [label="induction-case"];
  "R13" -> "NROOT" [label="induction-case"];
  "R5" -> "NROOT" [label="induction-case"];
  "R7" -> "NROOT" [label="induction-case"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" -> "NROOT" [label="induction-common"];
}
